      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 636,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating bodies of water in winter. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater). It also exists in the atmosphere, soils, glaciers, and living organisms. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and transport of nutrients occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Structural component** – cells are ~70 % water; water maintains cell turgor and shape.<br>• **Regulation of temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and water circulation. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), manufacturing (solvent, coolant), recreation, and many more. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → groundwater → back to oceans.<br>• Influences weather patterns, ocean currents, and climate regulation. |\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) that can exist as ice, liquid, or vapor under Earth‑like conditions. Its unique physical and chemical properties—especially its ability to dissolve many substances and to store/release heat—make it the cornerstone of chemistry, biology, and the planet’s climate system. Without water, the chemistry of life as we know it could not occur."
